        "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14210206/observations/step-q115924-92400c2988d9c1d7334a.html",
        "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14210206/observations/step-q115924-92400c2988d9c1d7334a.png",
        "worked_solution": "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=10\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ So, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 10\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} & =2x\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 10 & =2x \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, at that particular moment we have $x=5\\text{ m}.$",
        "can_dont_know": false,
        "can_skip": false,
        "question": {
          "dom_id": "step-q115924",
          "name": "Question 4",
          "problem": "The sides (in meters) of a square are decreasing. At a particular moment, the rate of decrease of the area of the square is numerically equal to ten times the rate of decrease of the sides. What is the length of the sides of the square at that moment?",
          "worked_solution": "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=10\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ So, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 10\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} & =2x\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 10 & =2x \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, at that particular moment we have $x=5\\text{ m}.$",
          "calculator_instructions": "",
