        "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14210206/observations/step-q48381-6a62530f68b2008b77ba.html",
        "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14210206/observations/step-q48381-6a62530f68b2008b77ba.png",
        "worked_solution": "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=4\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$, so replacing $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =h\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 4\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} & =h\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 4 & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at that particular moment, we have that the length of the diagonals of the square is $4\\text{ feet}.$",
        "can_dont_know": false,
        "can_skip": false,
        "question": {
          "dom_id": "step-q48381",
          "name": "Question 11",
          "problem": "The sides (in feet) of a square are increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the square is numerically equal to four times the rate of increase of the diagonals. What is the length of the diagonals of the square at that moment?",
          "worked_solution": "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=4\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$, so replacing $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =h\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 4\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} & =h\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 4 & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at that particular moment, we have that the length of the diagonals of the square is $4\\text{ feet}.$",
          "calculator_instructions": "",
