        "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14210206/observations/step-q116055-d96152641c60bb3c7908.html",
        "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14210206/observations/step-q116055-d96152641c60bb3c7908.png",
        "worked_solution": "Let $p$ be the perimeter of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $p$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\np=2\\sqrt{2}h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each diagonal of the square is decreasing at a rate of $10\\sqrt{2}\\text{ mm}\\text{/min},$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-10\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into the above expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10\\sqrt{2})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=-40\\text{ mm/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the square decreases at $40\\text{ mm}/\\text{min}.$",
        "can_dont_know": false,
        "can_skip": false,
        "question": {
          "dom_id": "step-q116055",
          "name": "Question 15",
          "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14210206/assets/eb40860015e5404f3680b22835149ddc63baf80cd8e1f34e30f936308c3221e0.png)\n\nIf the diagonals of a square decrease at a rate of $10\\sqrt{2}\\text{ mm}\\text{/min}$, at what rate is the perimeter decreasing?",
          "worked_solution": "Let $p$ be the perimeter of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $p$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\np=2\\sqrt{2}h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each diagonal of the square is decreasing at a rate of $10\\sqrt{2}\\text{ mm}\\text{/min},$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-10\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into the above expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10\\sqrt{2})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=-40\\text{ mm/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the square decreases at $40\\text{ mm}/\\text{min}.$",
          "calculator_instructions": "",
