        "solution": {
          "status": "ambiguous",
          "reasoning": "The square's area is A=x^2, so dA/dt=2x(dx/dt)=2(12)(3)=72 mm²/s. Thus option e is mathematically correct. No answer key or grade for this question is captured; the worked example concerns a different question.",
          "confidence": 1,
          "answers": [
            {
              "key": "selection",
              "correct_value": "72{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}",
              "correct_option": "e",
              "value_type": "math",
              "wrong_value": "144{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}",
              "correct_keys": [],
              "wrong_keys": [],
              "ma_value": null,
              "ma_basis": "none",
              "ma_evidence": "",
              "disagrees": false
            }
          ]
        },
        "solution_binding": "ffb13ebeea4d9b293529d5a62601c431278e46d9f6bfd31d83c88702ab9ebc82",
        "accepted": true,
        "feedback": {
          "dom_id": "step-q24800",
          "name": "Question 1",
          "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14210206/assets/740d181b8e8eff44f0eb258c8b68f30d05f70445aefabd5c00fd68e570e825dd.png)\n\nThe sides of a square are increasing in length at a rate of $3\\text{ mm/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the square changing when the sides have a length of $12\\text{ mm}?$",
          "worked_solution": "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is increasing at a rate of $3\\,\\text{mm/s},$ so we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3.$ Also, when the sides are $12\\,\\text{mm},$ we have $x=12.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =3\\cdot 2(12)=72{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square increases at $72\\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$",
          "calculator_instructions": "",
