      {
        "key": "step-q2773",
        "question": {
          "dom_id": "step-q2773",
          "name": "Question 3",
          "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14210206/assets/fd63afc83917b365d1b91bf4b033be10b64c76a18e7aa16cb8019dc8ef197eb1.png)\n\nThe sides of a square increase in length at a rate of $6\\text{ cm/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the square increasing when the sides are $4\\text{ cm}$ long?",
          "worked_solution": "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is increasing at a rate of $6\\,\\text{cm/s},$ so we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=6.$ Also, when the sides are $4\\,\\text{cm},$ we have $x=4.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =(6)\\cdot 2(4)=48{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square increases at $48\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$",
          "calculator_instructions": "",
          "fields": [
            {
              "key": "selection",
              "type": "radio",
              "choices": [
