            }
          ],
          "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14210206/observations/step-q73320-8fb4c8b07c4d5702050f.html",
          "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14210206/observations/step-q73320-8fb4c8b07c4d5702050f.png",
          "worked_solution": "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate,  and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14210206/assets/d713fe1a9286ec36d413bda32cce5ea4327003e6cc4f8a40a45cb945166d68c1.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =24\\text{ m}{}^{2}\\text{/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{12}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "can_dont_know": false,
          "can_skip": false,
          "question": {
            "dom_id": "step-q73320",
            "name": "Question 7",
            "problem": "The area of a square increases at a rate of $24{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/hour}.$ If $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square respectively, then find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$, where $t$ is the time in hours.",
            "worked_solution": "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate,  and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14210206/assets/d713fe1a9286ec36d413bda32cce5ea4327003e6cc4f8a40a45cb945166d68c1.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =24\\text{ m}{}^{2}\\text{/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{12}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
            "calculator_instructions": "",
