      {
        "key": "step-q344457",
        "question": {
          "dom_id": "step-q344457",
          "name": "Question 10",
          "problem": "The area of a square increases at a rate of $20{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ Given that $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square, respectively, find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds, as a function of $x.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}",
          "worked_solution": "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate, and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14210206/assets/e2b8bf4ff974ba499167a4349c894ea3c4c87cc14c731cf806e47d58c8c6e027.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=20\\text{ }{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{10}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "calculator_instructions": "",
          "fields": [
            {
              "key": "field-1",
              "type": "blank",
              "dom_id": "freeResponseTextbox-1",
