          ],
          "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14210206/observations/step-q24805-dd55931aa2babfb4ef41.html",
          "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14210206/observations/step-q24805-dd55931aa2babfb4ef41.png",
          "worked_solution": "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each diagonal of the square is increasing at a rate of $1.5\\,\\text{mm/min},$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=1.5.\n$$\n\nAlso, when the diagonals are $4\\,\\text{mm},$ we have $h=4.$ Substituting this information into the above expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=1.5\\cdot 4=6{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square increases at $6\\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$",
          "can_dont_know": false,
          "can_skip": false,
          "question": {
            "dom_id": "step-q24805",
            "name": "Question 13",
            "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14210206/assets/197557b866b4a4a1c2c151c29cc529e12a5048b941223fe8e2eb4a7c4959223e.png)\n\nThe diagonals of a square increase in length at a rate of $1.5\\text{ mm/min}.$ At what rate is the area of the square changing when the diagonals are $4\\,\\text{mm}$ long?",
            "worked_solution": "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each diagonal of the square is increasing at a rate of $1.5\\,\\text{mm/min},$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=1.5.\n$$\n\nAlso, when the diagonals are $4\\,\\text{mm},$ we have $h=4.$ Substituting this information into the above expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=1.5\\cdot 4=6{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square increases at $6\\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$",
            "calculator_instructions": "",
