          "can_dont_know": false,
          "can_skip": false,
          "solution_evidence": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14210206/observations/history-solution-q-314510-f6b9c5793c2f9acc73cf.html",
          "difficulty": "hard",
          "knowledge_point_source_id": null,
          "kp_href": "",
          "source_grade": "Correct",
          "raw_html": "<div class=\"question\" id=\"question-314510\"><div class=\"questionHeader\"><span class=\"questionNumber\">16.</span><span class=\"helpButton\" questionid=\"314510\">?</span></div><div class=\"questionGraphicFrame\"><img class=\"questionGraphic\" src=\"/graphics/q-314510\"/></div><div class=\"questionText\">A laser technician is reducing the diagonals of a square aperture at a rate of <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"228\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 119.4%; position: relative;\" tabindex=\"0\"><mjx-math aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"MJX-TEX\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c32\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2E\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c34\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn><mjx-mtext class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-cA0\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c6D\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c6D\"></mjx-c></mjx-mtext><mjx-mtext class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2F\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c73\"></mjx-c></mjx-mtext><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2E\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml display=\"inline\" unselectable=\"on\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2.4</mn><mtext> mm</mtext><mtext>/s</mtext><mo>.</mo></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> At what rate is the area of the aperture decreasing when the diagonals are <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"229\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 119.4%; position: relative;\" tabindex=\"0\"><mjx-math aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"MJX-TEX\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c31\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c32\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2E\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c35\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn><mjx-mstyle><mjx-mspace style=\"width: 0.167em;\"></mjx-mspace></mjx-mstyle><mjx-mtext class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c6D\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c6D\"></mjx-c></mjx-mtext></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml display=\"inline\" unselectable=\"on\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>12.5</mn><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\"><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace></mstyle><mtext>mm</mtext></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> long each?<p></p>\n<p><em>Hint</em>: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.</p><div class=\"freeResponseLine\">The area is decreasing at a rate of\n<div ;=\"\" class=\"freeResponseTextbox\" id=\"freeResponseTextbox-1\" style=\"min-width:31px;\n                                        margin-left:4px;\n                                        margin-right:2px;\n                                        background:;\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"230\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 119.4%; position: relative;\" tabindex=\"0\"><mjx-math aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"MJX-TEX\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c33\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c30\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml display=\"inline\" unselectable=\"on\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>30</mn></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></div> <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"231\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 119.4%; position: relative;\" tabindex=\"0\"><mjx-math aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"MJX-TEX\"><mjx-msup><mjx-mtext class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c6D\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c6D\"></mjx-c></mjx-mtext><mjx-script style=\"vertical-align: 0.363em;\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\" size=\"s\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c32\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-script></mjx-msup><mjx-mtext class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2F\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c73\"></mjx-c></mjx-mtext><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2E\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml display=\"inline\" unselectable=\"on\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mtext>mm</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mtext>/s</mtext><mo>.</mo></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></div></div><div class=\"answerDetails\"><div class=\"questionDifficulty difficultyHard\">H</div><span class=\"answerCreated\">Wed, Oct 14th @ 10:17 AM</span><span class=\"answerDetailsSeparator\">|</span><span class=\"timeElpased\">Elapsed: 0:21</span><span class=\"answerResult\"><span style=\"color: green;\">Correct</span></span></div></div>"
        },
        "grade": "Correct"
      },
      {
        "key": "step-q344664",
        "question": {
          "dom_id": "step-q344664",
          "name": "Question 17",
          "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14210206/assets/ef685549b562c570914bc8b5b5066cb621e42c8536586b37467afc6c1eb6c194.png)\n\nAn adjustable square skylight is being expanded as part of a building renovation. The area of the skylight is increasing at a rate of $27{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate are the diagonals increasing when each diagonal measures $18\\,\\text{mm}?$\n\nThe diagonal is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} $\\text{mm/s}.$",
          "worked_solution": "Let $A$ be the area of the square skylight, and $h$ be the length of its diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the relationship between $A$ and $h$ with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the area of the square is increasing at a rate of $27{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=27.\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the diagonals when the diagonal has length $h=18.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{18}\\cdot 27 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{27}{18} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =1.5\\text{ mm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the diagonal of the skylight is increasing at a rate of \n$1.5\\text{ mm/s}.$",
          "calculator_instructions": "",
          "fields": [
